00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.940
HOST: Migrating to cloud can
be intimidating with a ton

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.260
of things to worry about.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.300
How can you build
a solid foundation

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.937
for a seamless migration?

00:00:07.937 --> 00:00:09.270
Well, keep watching to find out.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:12.742
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:21.090
Welcome to Cloud Migration 101,
where we share migration tips

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:21.720
and tricks.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.880
When planning your
migration to Google Cloud,

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.800
you need to understand
some concepts related

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.300
to cloud architecture.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.000
A poorly-planned foundation
can cause your business

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.479
to face delays,
confusion, downtime,

00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:35.700
and can put the success of
your cloud migration at risk.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.980
But today's episode, I
have brought a friend

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.810
from Solutions
Architect Team who

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:42.840
will walk us through building
our cloud foundations.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.765
Grasp as much as you can, and
I'll see you on the other side.

00:00:45.765 --> 00:00:47.670
TRAVIS: Hi, I'm Travis.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:50.350
When migrating from an
on-premises environment or colo

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.175
managed data center
to the cloud,

00:00:52.175 --> 00:00:53.550
there are many
things to consider

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.830
from the IT, engineering,
and business perspectives.

00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:59.400
Today, let's cover
the topics that an IT

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.060
manager needs to think about
when migrating to Google Cloud.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.430
There are a few questions
you want to ask yourself.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.740
First, how do your
existing business

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.980
and organizational structures
map to Google Cloud?

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:15.390
Second, who from your team will
control, administer, and audit

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.100
access to Google
Cloud resources?

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.450
Third, what are best practices
for managing multiple teams

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:26.170
working simultaneously on
multiple Google Cloud projects?

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:27.720
Let's start with
the first question.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.420
How do you model your existing
business and organizational

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:32.760
structures in Google Cloud?

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.440
You'll want to map
your existing lines

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.010
of reporting and communication
to your resource hierarchy.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.050
The resource hierarchy
provides structure

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:41.940
to your cloud
environment, determines

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:45.300
the way you are billed, and
establishes a security model

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.610
for granting roles
and permissions.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.880
It's like the skeleton for
all your cloud resources.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.330
For example, a Compute
Engine VM is a resource

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.540
which falls within a project
and folder, and all of these

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.610
are contained within
an organization.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:04.680
Now, how do you control
access to cloud resources?

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.940
Once the resource
hierarchy is in place,

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:08.370
you can apply
security permissions

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:09.870
at each of these levels.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.840
These permissions are then
inherited by any child objects

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.430
in the hierarchy.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:17.010
For example, if you get
full admin privileges

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.200
on Department Y's
folder, you will

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.810
have full admin privileges
on all of the projects

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.770
and resources below it.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:26.490
These hierarchies can be
as complex or as simple

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.680
as your organization requires.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.880
A member is who or what you
want to give permission to.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.910
A role is a collection
of specific permissions

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:39.950
to determine what operations
can be performed on a resource.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:45.030
And a policy binds one or more
members to a particular role.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:46.890
Once you have a
policy, you'll be

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:51.420
able to clearly define who
can do what on what resources.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:56.220
As a simple equation, members
plus roles equals policy.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.360
Let's take a specific example.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:01.440
An enterprise wants to create
a policy for controlling access

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.050
to all of their
virtual machines.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:09.030
They might use Instance Admin
and App Engine Admin roles.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.730
Then they can create
a Google group

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:15.030
for the Ops Team that
will manage the instances.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:19.140
And the policy would link the
members and the roles together.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.580
They could then create
a read-only policy,

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:24.270
including Log Viewer
and Instance Viewer

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.000
roles for their
support team members

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.300
and, perhaps, a policy
with the Pub/Sub Publisher

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:34.260
role for the service account
of one of their microservices.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.050
That would be three
separate policies.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.240
That's the relationship
between IAM

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.890
and the resources,
projects, and folders.

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:44.850
Now, once you have the
resources in Google Cloud,

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.610
the question comes, how
do your teams actually

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.170
interact with them?

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:52.440
There are four ways to interact
with Google Cloud Services,

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.930
and your IAM permissions
will control what

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.100
you can do with all of them.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.180
First, we have the cloud
console, the graphical user

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:02.190
interface that lets you
interact with Google

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.290
Cloud within your browser.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.870
Second, we have
the cloud APIs that

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.420
provide programmatic
interfaces for interacting

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:12.570
with Google Cloud from
your application code.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:16.950
Third, the cloud SDK, which is
a developer-friendly software

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.800
library that enables your
applications to interact

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.180
with Cloud APIs.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.390
And lastly, we have the
G Cloud command-line tool

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:27.000
that lets you make
calls to the cloud API

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.830
directly from your command line.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.160
HOST: That was great, Travis.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:30.660
Thank you.

